//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// The relation: "data can get from topology A to topology B." It holds between
// two values, not of either one, so it is exactly the kind of fact a
// non-relational analysis cannot record.
//
// The function below is generic over three devices and never names one. It
// states the relations it needs in its signature -- `where Reachable<A, B>,
// Reachable<B, C>` -- and each call site discharges them against the declared
// transfer graph. The relation is written once and checked at every boundary
// it crosses, instead of being re-derived (or lost) inside each instantiation.
//
// Here the second hop is the broken one: `Island` declares memory with no
// transfer edge into it, so nothing can reach it.
//
// Run:
//   vxc www/book/src/correlation/03_reachability_across_a_generic_call.vx
//
// Expected:
//   Error: unsatisfied `where Reachable<B, C>` in call to 'pipeline':
//   no transfer path from CPU to Custom("Island")
//
// The failing constraint is named. The first hop, GPU -> CPU, is fine and is
// not reported.
//
//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//

// A device whose memory nothing can reach: no `transfer` edge is declared.
Topology Island {
  memory: Memory::IslandRAM
}

fn pipeline<A: Topology, B: Topology, C: Topology>(
  a: Pinned<i32, Topology::A>,
  b: Pinned<i32, Topology::B>,
  c: Pinned<i32, Topology::C>
) -> i32
where Reachable<A, B>, Reachable<B, C>
{
  return 0;
}

fn main() -> i32 {
  let ga = spawn on(Topology::GPU) { 1 };
  let cb = spawn on(Topology::CPU) { 2 };
  let ic = spawn on(Topology::Island) { 3 };
  // A = GPU, B = CPU, C = Island. GPU -> CPU holds; CPU -> Island does not.
  pipeline(ga, cb, ic);
  return 0;
}
